# Split to PRs

Turn one pile of work into the smallest feasible pull requests.

Follow {{.Rule "git"}} for fetches, comparisons, worktree safety, staging, signed commits, and pushes. Use {{.Skill "cleanup-git"}} for branch and worktree cleanup. Use {{.Skill "pr"}} for every pull request title, body, and GitHub write.

## Create independent or dependent pull requests

Create one pull request for each smallest logically shippable slice that can pass continuous integration at its branch position. Create independent sibling branches from trunk by default. Use GitHub stacking only when two or more slices depend on each other. An installed extension or an existing stack does not change those dependencies.

Use the upstream {{.Skill "gh-stack"}} for dependent stacks and the execution steps in section 3 for each split.

## Reuse before recreate

When an open PR already covers the work being split, repurpose that PR as the stack base when doing so preserves its intent and unique work. Do not abandon the existing PR merely to create a cleaner stack.

## Stack position in titles

After all PRs in the stack have GitHub numbers, read the numbers bottom to top.

If the numbers are consecutive integers in stack order, leave titles unchanged.

If the numbers are non-sequential, append ` (PR x/N)` to every title in the stack. Use `x` as the 1-based bottom-to-top position and `N` as the stack size. Apply the suffix through the `pr` skill edit path (`gh pr edit`).

Put the suffix at the very end of the title, after the `[TICKET-12345] ...` text from the `pr` skill.

Single-PR stacks (`N` is 1) never get the suffix.

Example: `[AGENCY-11267] Add plaid relink URL (PR 2/3)`

## Hard rules

- Continue through branch creation, commits, pushes, and pull request creation without pausing.
- Create the smallest slices that are each logically shippable and can pass every required check at their branch position.
- Group changes only when separating them would break required checks, omit required behavior, or create an unusable intermediate state.
- When an open PR exists, follow **Reuse before recreate** above.
- Follow {{.Rule "git"}} for all repository mutations and worktree handling.
- A recoverable snapshot preserves work. It does not require leaving an existing open PR untouched after a split request.
- Always save a recoverable snapshot before moving work around. This often starts from dirty work on `main`, so do not assume there is already a safe branch.
- Snapshot refs must stay under `refs/backup/`, never `refs/heads/`.
- Every pull request title, body, and GitHub write follows {{.Skill "pr"}}.

## 1. Check the state

Compare the current work to the repo's default branch, including committed and uncommitted changes. Summarize the real slices you see, and use the chat history to recover intent.

Before dividing the work, find ownership signals for the touched paths (`CODEOWNERS`, nested ownership files, `tools/ownership/PRODUCTOWNERS`, or repo equivalents). Use them to identify natural reviewer boundaries.

Check for existing open PRs that already cover this work:

```bash
gh pr list --head "$(git branch --show-current)" --state open --json number,title,url,headRefName
```

For each PR number returned, run `gh pr view <number> --json body,title,url,state,number`.

Record every open PR number, branch, and URL. If the user mentioned a PR by number, confirm it with `gh pr view <number>`.

Classify each dependency between slices. Determine dependencies from the behavior each slice requires.

When `git worktree list` shows more than one checkout, record which path owns trunk before dividing the work:

```bash
git worktree list
```

Follow {{.Rule "git"}} for trunk and worktree safety.

## 2. Build the split map

Write the pull request titles and their branch relationships, then continue immediately. Add a one-line scope note only when a title is unclear. Show a Mermaid diagram only when it clarifies a dependency.

Split at every logical boundary where the lower slice and remaining work can each stay shippable and pass required checks. Keep tests with the behavior they prove. Keep a migration with the minimum compatible application change. Keep generated output with the source change that produces it.

Use ownership boundaries as additional split signals. Do not combine independently shippable behavior merely because one reviewer owns both areas. When stacking is necessary, order foundations before consumers.

Default to independent PRs off the default branch. Stack PRs only when the dependency is real.

Repurpose an existing open PR as the stack base when it covers the work and preserves its intent. Thin that branch to the bottom slice. Replace and close the PR only when the new split preserves all its intent and unique work. Honor any existing instruction to leave the PR untouched. Otherwise, reuse or replace it.

Use GitHub stacking for dependent slices and plain Git sibling branches for independent slices.

## 3. Execute the split

### Save the work

If there is uncommitted work, save a recoverable snapshot without changing the working tree:

```bash
SHA=$(git stash create "pre-split")
if [ -n "$SHA" ]; then
  git update-ref "refs/backup/pre-split-$(date +%s)" "$SHA"
fi
```

For each split-map entry:

1. Reuse and thin its recorded branch when the entry covers an existing PR. Create a dependent branch from its recorded parent. Create only an independent branch from the remote trunk.
2. Use the branch's owning worktree to stage named paths and create a signed commit. Verify the commit under the Git rule. Push independent branches through the Git workflow.
3. Create or update each independent pull request through the `pr` workflow.

### Submit dependent branches

1. Run stack commands from a suitable feature checkout. Do not switch the main checkout. Configure `rerere.enabled=true`, then adopt the recorded chain with `gh stack init <bottom-branch> <next-branch> ... --base <trunk>`. Use explicit branch names in bottom-to-top order. Preserve existing branches and pull request numbers.
2. Inspect `gh stack view --json` and confirm the recorded chain. Set `remote.pushDefault` to the intended remote when the repository has multiple remotes.
3. Submit the verified commits with `gh stack submit --auto`. Apply the `pr` workflow to every resulting title and body before marking drafts ready for review. Confirm each existing pull request retains its number and each base matches the recorded parent.

## 4. Report back

Keep it short: PR titles and URLs, plus anything left on the starting branch or working tree. For dependent splits, include the bottom pull request URL and stack order. Before reporting, confirm stack PR numbers are sequential bottom to top. When they are not, apply **Stack position in titles** so each reported title includes ` (PR x/N)`. Per-PR final output (Slack one-liner and URL) comes from the `pr` skill Step 8. Do not delete the backup ref or original branch unless the user asks.
